  "a2-s11": {
    id: "a2-s11", name: "Colonial assemblies and the Dominion of New England", intro: "Use the passage below to answer the questions that follow.",
    sources: [{ title: "Source", blocks: [
      { p: "Across the British colonies, settlers expected to govern themselves through elected assemblies. Virginia's House of Burgesses first met in 1619, and New England towns ran their own affairs through town meetings. When James II joined the northern colonies into the Dominion of New England in 1686, abolished their assemblies, and put them under a royal governor, Sir Edmund Andros, colonists resented it deeply. News of the Glorious Revolution in England in 1689 set off uprisings that toppled Andros. In the decades that followed, Britain's loose enforcement of its trade laws, later called salutary neglect, let colonial assemblies gain control over taxes and spending and grow used to managing their own affairs." },
      { cite: PRACTICE, kind: "practice" },
    ] }],
  },
